{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium","id":1554669,"title":"Are Children resistant to the Delirium?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-10-25T14:35:25-07:00","id":1554669,"title":"Are Children resistant to the Delirium?","content":"Due to their open mindedness, innocence and inability to comprehend the fear of death can Children resist the Delirium?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-25T15:56:19-07:00","id":1554671,"title":"","content":"No. No matter how naive a human is, they still have the Night Fear in their bones.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-25T16:33:31-07:00","id":1554673,"title":"","content":"Well, it allows them to see Changelings and Wraiths.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-25T18:37:06-07:00","id":1554676,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samuelalpha86</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554673#post1554673\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, it allows them to see Changelings and Wraiths.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not related.<br /> <br /> The Delirium isn't about a closed mind, it's about the Garou being terrific giant killing machines with a history of going full gore on humans so intense it became instinctual.<br /> <br /> As many things in Werewolf, it runs on themes of evolution, nature, animal instinct, and spiritual inheritance.<br /> <br /> Humans suffer the Delirium because they're humans, not as in people, but as in <i>Homo Sapiens</i>.<br /> <br /> On another note: children can and do comprehend the most basic forms of the fear of death, which is the fear of violence and of being eaten. Werewolves are stupidly scary animals. Children definitely fear them.<br /> <br /> While some children can be quite fearless face a lot of things we take for granted to be dangerous, that's not because of any inherent inability to fear death-related dangers.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-25T20:54:50-07:00","id":1554678,"title":"","content":"To come at it from a slightly different direction:<br /> <br /> The WoD tends to give STs the <b>option</b> of having children notice the supernatural as a thematic part of the game. There's no game where children are mechanically automatically capable of piercing supernatural effects. This is purely for atmosphere. Is a normal child with an imaginary friend just a normal child doing a normal child thing, or are they interacting with a supernatural normally invisible to normal humans? Is that creepy child just having a goth/emo phase, or have they been haunted for as long as they can remember and accept ghosts are real and have to cope with it since nobody believes them? This is all stock trope and genre dressing in the source media the WoD was built from. The WoD is not a setting where the innocence and naivety of children is a source of hope or positivity... but rather a source of horror because it just makes children that much more targets of the darkness.<br /> <br /> So... does being immune to Delirium help that? Not IMO. People at risk of Delirium are seeing a giant murder monster openly acting. Children acting &quot;normally&quot; would largely just act like they were under Delirium anyway: terror. A child that sits around calmly as a pack of Garou rip a squad of fomori guards to shreds is not making the WoD a more Gothic-Dark sort of setting. It's turning kids into already broken people.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-25T23:36:37-07:00","id":1554680,"title":"","content":"By the way, an important aspect of the game is that being immune to Delirium doesn't make the experience of witnessing a Crinos any better. Kinfolk are allies, but they're terrified of being near a Garou in anything but Homid, because they're still giant and angry killing machines that can turn someone to pieces even by accident.<br /> <br /> While they're able to function in the presence of Garou, at the end of the day being immune to the Delirium is more a source of trauma and probably PTSD for kinfolk than anything positive in their lives. Not to say that kinfolk children already go through this, and you can have children who are still uninformed or even lost kin, so you don't really need to give this immunity to normal children.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-26T02:03:56-07:00","id":1554681,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samuelalpha86</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium#post1554669\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Due to their open mindedness, innocence and inability to comprehend the fear of death can Children resist the Delirium?</div> </div> </div> </div>Short answer: no<br /> <br /> Long answer: No, buuuut\u2026<br /> <br /> So, Delirium is the overwhelming instinctive fear that affects human beings who see werewolves and some other Fera in their war forms<br /> Kinfolk, Ghouls, Vampires, Risen, etc, everything human-adjacent are immune<br /> <br /> The idea is, thee collective unconscious remembers those millennia of terror of the Impergium so majority of humans panic and run, or collapse into a catatonic fear. Even those who can control themselves will forget the encounter later, either by rationalizing what they saw as a bear or by forgetting the whole incident. This includes if the shapeshifter shifts in the presence of someone - yet curiosuly the Crawerling form of the Ananasi or the Microchiptera form of the Camazotz for some reason don't trigger this (frankly, a swarm of houndreds or thousands of tiny spiders are far more frighenting for many than a Drider-like monster, but\u2026 )<br /> Photographs, video (live or recorded), or other such evidence won\u2019t trigger any fear reaction and the Human even rationalize it is fake unless have a very Strong willpower (8 and above\u2026 an avarage Human usually have 3, four at best)<br /> <br /> Now the trick is, it is not an evolutionary trait<br /> The Rokea pointed this out marvelously:<br /> <br /> &quot;This is a strange line of defense, indeed \u2014 to avoid a predator by denying its existence? A fish that denied our existence would find itself swallowed in seconds. I do not pretend to understand it. Perhaps this denial makes a statement not only about the prey but also about the predator. Perhaps not, though \u2014 after all, though we had no part in this purge, our war form sends humans running.&quot;<br /> <br /> This is the nail right on the head; this is not something that developed as a deffense-mechanism<br /> It is more of a supernatural effect that kicked in later<br /> <br /> The Kitusne is a later eddition, appeared in the last days of the Fourth Age when Bai Mianxi, the White-Faced One made a bargain with Gaia for the safety of her people; they don't participated in the Impergium therefore they do not trigger the Delirium.<br /> Except the Rokea not really participated either - and yes, you can say, it is just their version, but it is hard to argue with the fact the Sharks not really ventured inland, nor took Humans as breedin stock - a crucial and horrific part of the Impergium - and as immortals most of them were alive that time even if their memories are not the best, you don't really forget you took part as a race in such a massive murderfest; they ad plenty of those and remember them keenly\u2026<br /> The Nuwisha didn't induce Delirium, but rather were invisible to human eyes - This was changed to very reduced Delirium.<br /> The other end of this spectrum are the Mokol\u00e9 with their hilarious 7 point merit &quot;Veiled to the Garou&quot; - The Mokole and the Kings hunted and slew the Garou and their mammalian ancestors for ages. There are a few Mokole who retain that ancient aura of mind-shattering terror. The Mokole with this advantage causes the Delirium in Garou<br /> <br /> To make things even more amusing, the Garou have a 3 point Flaw, Pierced Veil - Your Crinos form does not trigger the Delirium<br /> <br /> <br /> Okay, so we nailed down, it s not some evolutionary trait, may or may not rooted in attoricities against your kind (more-or-less explaining why certain aboriginal cultures whose ancestors weren't part of the Impergium also do not trigger Delirium\u2026) and have a heavy supernatural element in it if not supernatural altogether<br /> <br /> So by that logic?<br /> Eh... no? Yes? Kinda? Maybeeee?<br /> <br /> The Delirium-chart is pretty straightforward<br /> 1 Willpower - Catatonic Fear kicks in, and the Human forgets shortly after whats happened<br /> 2 Willpower - Panic kicks in and the human bolts, trying to put as much distance between himself and the Garou as possible and the Human forgets shortly after whats happened<br /> 3 Willpower - Disbelief shuts down the brain, the human retreats to a corner to avoid the \u201challucination\u201d until it passes, but doesn\u2019t collapse in fear and the Human forgets shortly after whats happened<br /> 4 Willpower - The experience triggers a Berserk behavior, the Human attacks the &quot;monster&quot; and the Human forgets shortly after whats happened (if survived this experience)<br /> 5 Willpower - Terror grips the mind, it is like Panic, except with rational thought so the Huma can think and flee and the Human forgets shortly after whats happened<br /> 6 Willpower - Conciliatory behavior; &quot;Dormamu, I've Come To Bargain!&quot;you Will try to plead and bargain to survive and if survived, the Human forgets shortly after whats happened<br /> 7 Willpower - Controlled Fear; although terrified, he does not panic. The human will flee or fight as appropriate, but remains in control of his actions. The Human Will not forget whats happened but but will rationalize to explain<br /> 8 Willpower - Curiosity kicks in, proving survival was probably huamnity's dumpstat; they remember what they saw (more or less), and they might well investigate the matter further<br /> 9 Willpower - Something snaps or boils up from the collective memory and the Human ends up angry and afraid, do not forget ,clealry remembers what saw\u2026 and probably even try to hunt it down<br /> 10 Willpower - Not really realistic but in theory the human is not the slightest bit afraid or bothered by the Garou. Even Kinfolk aren\u2019t this stoic, so Garou tend to be very suspicious of such folks\u2026 and probably kills them for good measures.<br /> <br /> So, what about a child-like innocence and inability to comprehend the fear of death\u2026?<br /> This is not really true children can comprehend fear, and this ability develops from infancy through observation and social cues. Even very young babies pay attention to caregivers' fearful expressions and can learn to be afraid by watching their reactions. As they grow, their understanding of fear expands to include things like strangers and later, more abstract concepts like monsters or war.<br /> A child's comprehension of death develops over time, with understanding of finality typically emerging between ages 5 and 7. Before this, young children often see death as temporary, and may repeatedly ask when the person will return. By age 7, most understand death is final<br /> <br /> A three and a hlaf meters tall werewolf with claws and fangs not really trigger the &quot;Puppy!!!&quot; reaction<br /> Boo was a 2-year-old toddler who was curious and naive and not really afraid but developed a bond with Sulley and Mike in Monsters, Inc.<br /> Actually this is a very good starting point; a very young child may not be effected with the Delirium - ST descretion whats benefits the story and game the best - but highlights the fact kids be young or older indeed do fear the monsters under the bed, in the dark, in the closet<br /> I would say around 5 years old the Delirium would hit them like a truck - before that? Eh... depends?<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-26T04:45:08-07:00","id":1554683,"title":"","content":"No. In my opinion, children are not immune to the Delirium. They are just as scared as adults.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-26T10:41:42-07:00","id":1554684,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554681#post1554681\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Now the trick is, it is not an evolutionary trait<br /> The Rokea pointed this out marvelously:<br /> <br /> &quot;This is a strange line of defense, indeed \u2014 to avoid a predator by denying its existence? A fish that denied our existence would find itself swallowed in seconds. I do not pretend to understand it. Perhaps this denial makes a statement not only about the prey but also about the predator. Perhaps not, though \u2014 after all, though we had no part in this purge, our war form sends humans running.&quot;<br /> <br /> This is the nail right on the head; this is not something that developed as a deffense-mechanism<br /> It is more of a supernatural effect that kicked in later</div> </div> </div> </div>More that the speaker who claimed this in the Rokea book didn't have a fully in-depth knowledge of biology, though at the time, what was more likely going on (super generational trauma) had yet to be published.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Kitusne is a later eddition, appeared in the last days of the Fourth Age when Bai Mianxi, the White-Faced One made a bargain with Gaia for the safety of her people; they don't participated in the Impergium therefore they do not trigger the Delirium.<br /> Except the Rokea not really participated either - and yes, you can say, it is just their version, but it is hard to argue with the fact the Sharks not really ventured inland, nor took Humans as breedin stock - a crucial and horrific part of the Impergium - and as immortals most of them were alive that time even if their memories are not the best, you don't really forget you took part as a race in such a massive murderfest; they ad plenty of those and remember them keenly\u2026 </div> </div> </div>Rokea probably tap into a thread similar to thalassophobia. What keeps large, dangerous sharks from being more terrifying is that they can't go on land. Well, pop quiz, hot shot - now you're face to face with a shark that can walk. What do you do now? Well, before anything else, you shit bricks.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Nuwisha didn't induce Delirium, but rather were invisible to human eyes - This was changed to very reduced Delirium. </div> </div> </div>Probably because one eventually had to ask, &quot;hey, if you're invisible by default to humans in this form, why don't you actually do something?&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The other end of this spectrum are the Mokol\u00e9 with their hilarious 7 point merit &quot;Veiled to the Garou&quot; - The Mokole and the Kings hunted and slew the Garou and their mammalian ancestors for ages. There are a few Mokole who retain that ancient aura of mind-shattering terror. The Mokole with this advantage causes the Delirium in Garou<br /> <br /> To make things even more amusing, the Garou have a 3 point Flaw, Pierced Veil - Your Crinos form does not trigger the Delirium </div> </div> </div>Merits are kind of a grab bag of special cases, though for the Mokole, they flat-out confessed to having carved humans out of earlier mammals to arrive at the neotenous ape figurines of never-ending tool use that are humans; all this, and all through picking who lived and who died! Unlike the Garou, this actually is going to be evolutionary, because they did it since about the time of the K-T boundary event up until almost the present (on a geologic scale, the gap is nothing). What's wrong with &quot;Veiled to Garou&quot; should be that they should be veiled to anything with a modicum of ancestral connection to humans.<br /> <br /> Pierced Veil, meanwhile, has an almost pure gamist approach to the material. &quot;I'm giving up something to get more points.&quot; I'm not sure that it interacts with the diegesis very much, or is much good in a discussion.<br /> <br /> Finally, it's worth noting that supernatural terror effects can induce an effect so similar that they just use the same name - a lot of possessed can do this, even to beings that wouldn't normally be susceptible. This is also the default reaction to seeing a nexus crawler. However, I don't think we can actually say that all things with the same mechanics for rules have the same origin, because Wasp Talons uses Firearms to hit, and, well, it's pretty distinct from a gun that got milled and assembled, or the bullets that said gun fires off.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Okay, so we nailed down, it s not some evolutionary trait, may or may not rooted in attoricities against your kind (more-or-less explaining why certain aboriginal cultures whose ancestors weren't part of the Impergium also do not trigger Delirium\u2026) and have a heavy supernatural element in it if not supernatural altogether </div> </div> </div>Nah, it could, as I noted, be rooted in generational trauma. Very, very heavy generational trauma, as the effects of 3000 years of constant hunting don't wear off in just slightly over 9000.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:8px\">(crushes scouter)</span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A three and a hlaf meters tall werewolf with claws and fangs not really trigger the &quot;Puppy!!!&quot; reaction </div> </div> </div>Three and a half? Golgol Fangs-Firsts don't grow on trees!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Boo was a 2-year-old toddler who was curious and naive and not really afraid but developed a bond with Sulley and Mike in Monsters, Inc.<br /> Actually this is a very good starting point; a very young child may not be effected with the Delirium - ST descretion whats benefits the story and game the best - but highlights the fact kids be young or older indeed do fear the monsters under the bed, in the dark, in the closet<br /> I would say around 5 years old the Delirium would hit them like a truck - before that? Eh... depends? </div> </div> </div>The other part is... a baby cries and screams a lot. Under the influence of the Delirium, the same baby... would probably also just scream and cry a lot. You probably couldn't tell a baby in a diaper that needs changing from a baby suffering Delirium. An early juvenile human with better movement capacity, like a toddler, probably has a more noticeable reaction, and that would probably be to run away, hide, and not respond until hours after. They would also promptly forget the specifics of the event if they lived but the trauma would likely persist through their life.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-26T11:52:40-07:00","id":1554685,"title":"","content":"If we're going to get into development psychology and how children react, I think the biggest thing to change in how Delirium would work would be that younger children &quot;fantasize&quot; instead of rationalize. That is, younger children don't have a strong coherent mental model of the world for major new events to fit into. They can't rationalize as we picture the concept because new stimuli aren't challenging a strong set of presuppositions that require explaining the challenge, nor do they have the tools for that yet anyway. So they're going to make something up, that could range from odd by plausible to wildly fantastic but wildly inaccurate to what actually happened.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-26T17:42:54-07:00","id":1554689,"title":"","content":"Some things I want to comment.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554681#post1554681\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Photographs, video (live or recorded), or other such evidence won\u2019t trigger any fear reaction and the Human even rationalize it is fake unless have a very Strong willpower (8 and above\u2026 an avarage Human usually have 3, four at best)</div> </div> </div> </div>IIRC recordings, photographs, etc. don't induce Delirium at all. Humans will rationalize because it's quite stupid to believe those things (and some people will believe, but they're usually quite stupid people). It's always important to remember that the rules are about the supernatural effect, but there are still perfectly natural reactions that won't simply be the opposite of the supernatural effect. They'll actually be pretty similar when we're talking the Delirium.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">rationalizing what they saw as a bear</div> </div> </div> </div>Correction, as a big animal.<br /> <br /> If you rationalize it as a bear in Brazil, there's something really wrong with you (well, except if you're not from here, that may be interesting to see in a game, how foreigners rationalize).<br /> <br /> That's a small thing, but still important. We don't all live in the US, so this kind of detail sometimes stings.<br /> <br /> And even in the US the idea of bears everywhere is misplaced. At least 10 states have no bear population, and on most others bears aren't found all over the place.<br /> <br /> Big animals vary a lot depending on where you live, and they may not be an option at all, with preference given to &quot;a dude in a fantasy&quot; or something like that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">(frankly, a swarm of houndreds or thousands of tiny spiders are far more frighenting for many than a Drider-like monster, but\u2026 )</div> </div> </div> </div>But that's about the supernatural effect and the forgetfulness attached. So this only means that the person won't follow the Delirium chart.<br /> <br /> Most people will probably still freak out by the sight, with the difference that no preternatural effect will make them forget or rationalize.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now the trick is, it is not an evolutionary trait</div> </div> </div> </div>No, but we must distinguish between evolution as a technical term and evolution as a theme for the story, and it still fits the later.<br /> <br /> This is not so much about being an adaptation (although that can be argued), but about fitting the theme of a species-wide changed triggered by past experiences.<br /> <br /> But yes, at the end of the day this is, like everything else in WtA, a &quot;spiritual&quot; effect, not a biological one.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Except the Rokea not really participated either</div> </div> </div> </div>Didn't they?<br /> <br /> <b>A lot</b> of people live near the sea, always had. And some sharks do venture a certain distance through rivers. Humans need water for a lot of things, and some of those things do include going into the water (we take baths since always), so water-borne predators are a problem, always have been. Sharks are, indeed, one of our greatest fears way before <i>Jaws</i>.<br /> <br /> Not to say that ancient Rokhea didn't necessarily had the same aversion to land as modern ones have. It doesn't even make sense for them to be half-human if they disregarded dry land so thoroughly in the past.<br /> <br /> Between the actual age of most current Rokhea (a few centuries at best) and their not-so-perfect memories, it's unwise to put so much stock on their ideas about how things were several thousands years ago.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">and as immortals most of them were alive that time</div> </div> </div> </div>Were, they? I'm not so sure.<br /> <br /> Rokhea are still plenty violent and seek dangerous combat more often than maybe they should. Even if they don't age, it isn't likely that your average wereshark lives more than a century or two, if that much.<br /> <br /> The game works with greater stretches of time than even Vampire when talking about the &quot;legendary past&quot;. Even for those who don't age, the Impergium happened just too long ago. It's just too much for anything at all to be reliable. It was so long ago that, despite everyone knowing the stories, no one really puts dates on them except for those with a bent towards natural history, it's simply a Mythic Past.<br /> <br /> Even personal memory doesn't count for much at this scale. Memory is unreliable, it's unreliable for a normal human in a few decades, imagine how it is for someone that lived almost ten thousand years, for example. I'd say anything older than 500 years is as good as a dream for the Rokhea, even if they don't feel that way (we generally don't feel that way about our own memory even it being far worse than we would like).<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">merit</div> </div> </div> </div>As others said, take great care when dealing with Merits and Flaws.<br /> <br /> They are exceptions, not rules. At best they're relatively rare occurrences, at worse they're regarded as non-existent at all unless happening at the table. Take for example <b><i>Unbondable</i></b>. No NPC I can remember ever had it. No fluff ever mentioned someone with it. For all intents and purposes, if a character in your game have this, they may well be the first such case ever. At the very least they're so exceedingly rare that most Elders never heard of anything like that.<br /> <br /> What a Merit/Flaw means for the lore is a very hard thing to define, but as a rule of thumb they can be written off as at least uncommon, no matter how cheap or useful they are to get. A typical character for the setting is someone without any of them.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is not really true children can comprehend fear</div> </div> </div> </div>Too young children can't comprehend fear (as they can't comprehend nothing to begin with, and fear is a lot harder to comprehend than it seems), they can <b>feel</b> it. And they can feel it since the very beginning, because it's hardwired in the brain.<br /> <br /> What happens here is that the whole &quot;comprehend&quot; perspective is irrelevant. This is not about comprehension.<br /> <br /> A baby fears pain. They fear discomfort. They fear a lot of things that don't even need to make sense for an older child, much less an adult. Their lack of experiences and abstraction means they may fear a lot of things we don't, and not fear a lot of things <b>we</b> can't comprehend how they don't, but the basic emotion of fear is pretty much universal. IRL a baby without fear has some neurological problem (and possibly a very serious one, although they may also just &quot;appear&quot; without fear because their caregivers don't understand what's going on).<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">A three and a hlaf meters tall werewolf</div> </div> </div> </div>Will cause <i>Delirium</i> even on other werewolves, because that's seriously bigger than a one-story house.<br /> <br /> A typical Crinos is 2,74 meters (9 feet). 3,5 meters would be about 11 feet 6 inch, if I calculated it correctly (seriously, using feet with inches is weird as hell).<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Boo was a 2-year-old toddler who was curious and naive</div> </div> </div> </div>And who did felt fear when she saw Sully doing something actually scary. <b>A lot</b> of fear, and she had already bonded with him.<br /> <br /> Most monsters from <i>Monster Inc</i> weren't really scary to begin with, they were wonky and colorful, and only managed to scare children because they overtly trained to do it.<br /> <br /> Even then, Boo was not a regular child. She was evidently abnormal in her courage, so she's not a good comparison to how children typically behave.<br /> <br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554684#post1554684\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">though for the Mokole, they flat-out confessed to having carved humans out of earlier mammals to arrive at the neotenous ape figurines of never-ending tool use that are humans; all this, and all through picking who lived and who died! Unlike the Garou, this actually is going to be evolutionary, because they did it since about the time of the K-T boundary event up until almost the present (on a geologic scale, the gap is nothing). What's wrong with &quot;Veiled to Garou&quot; should be that they should be veiled to anything with a modicum of ancestral connection to humans.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe, maybe not.<br /> <br /> Since the Mokole didn't need humans to begin with as their other-half, it's possible that other Changers are also older than humanity. Everything considered about how any deviation from baseline human renders you immune to the <i>Delirium</i>, I'd say its unlikely that this is because of their actions being older and more that it has another explanation, specific to Garou.<br /> <br /> A question that arises: did the Mokole cause <i>Delirium</i> on dinosaurs? Keeping in mind that they're lizard-changers, not dinosaur-changers, and while this was not knew at the time of their publication, dinosaurs aren't reptiles. There were at the time big enough gators to feed on T-Rex, so that's a possibility.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">You probably couldn't tell a baby in a diaper that needs changing from a baby suffering Delirium.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually you probably could if you have experience with babies.<br /> <br /> Parents discern all the time why the baby is crying. Hunger, fear, anxiety, full diapers, they all produce distinct reactions, even if they're all on the same ballpark of screaming and crying.<br /> <br /> That said, yes, it would be just another iteration of screaming and crying. Except maybe the baby would instead paralyze, they sometimes do that in fear, specially intense fear.<br /> <br /> It would also be impossible to meaningfully distinguish between <i>Delirium</i> and natural fear from a fucking werewolf.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">the trauma would likely persist through their life.</div> </div> </div> </div>That may or may not be the case.<br /> <br /> One possible interpretation of <i>Delirium</i> is that it protects the brain precisely from this level of trauma.<br /> <br /> You forget or write it off as something else entirely. You won't ever actually remember what happened. PTSD in particular is all about not being able to <u>forget</u>, so being <b>forced</b> to forget may be a good thing.<br /> <br /> I'm particularly prone to this interpretation because it increases the impact of any effect that <i>removes</i> the <i>Delirium</i> or otherwise forces the person to remember, or of just being immune to it, and how this may be good for the player, but is a curse for the character.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-27T08:31:30-07:00","id":1554697,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554689#post1554689\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Maybe, maybe not.<br /> <br /> Since the Mokole didn't need humans to begin with as their other-half, it's possible that other Changers are also older than humanity. Everything considered about how any deviation from baseline human renders you immune to the <i>Delirium</i>, I'd say its unlikely that this is because of their actions being older and more that it has another explanation, specific to Garou.<br /> <br /> A question that arises: did the Mokole cause <i>Delirium</i> on dinosaurs? Keeping in mind that they're lizard-changers, not dinosaur-changers, and while this was not knew at the time of their publication, dinosaurs aren't reptiles. There were at the time big enough gators to feed on T-Rex, so that's a possibility.</div> </div> </div> </div>If we're permitted Shattered Dreams as an explanation, Mokole used to be non-avian dinosaurs and other mesozoic fauna in their normal animal forms, but those all died out and they had to jump over using high-end rites or a Gift. They're crocodilians and lizards nowadays, but those are the ones left over.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <br /> That may or may not be the case.<br /> <br /> One possible interpretation of <i>Delirium</i> is that it protects the brain precisely from this level of trauma.<br /> <br /> You forget or write it off as something else entirely. You won't ever actually remember what happened. PTSD in particular is all about not being able to <u>forget</u>, so being <b>forced</b> to forget may be a good thing.<br /> <br /> I'm particularly prone to this interpretation because it increases the impact of any effect that <i>removes</i> the <i>Delirium</i> or otherwise forces the person to remember, or of just being immune to it, and how this may be good for the player, but is a curse for the character. </div> </div> </div>The books kind of jump around on this, too. It might be conflating it with the Curse when it does.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-27T12:40:29-07:00","id":1554698,"title":"","content":"I think it's fair to say that the books don't have a single cohesive answer to the broader issue of why Delirium even happens; even within a given edition. It seems easier and more practical to just leave it to the basics (Delirium is a supernatural effect some Shifters cause on normal humans) and consider anything else unreliable in-universe received wisdom.<br /> <br /> We can, of course, come up with functional explanations that probably do a better job than the books current offer, such that an explanation being held in-character would be more satisfying. But that's going a bit beyond the issue of how children relate to the effect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-27T21:51:28-07:00","id":1554713,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554697#post1554697\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If we're permitted Shattered Dreams as an explanation, Mokole used to be non-avian dinosaurs and other mesozoic fauna in their normal animal forms, but those all died out and they had to jump over using high-end rites or a Gift. They're crocodilians and lizards nowadays, but those are the ones left over.</div> </div> </div> </div>I didn't read SD yet, and it has its own problems, but I know the idea of them being dinosaurs was the original point and the book plays to that.<br /> <br /> But it's not only a contrived thing, and possibly less interesting (at least it is for me), most importantly it doesn't really means they were every dinosaur species, or that every dinosaur was kinfolk.<br /> <br /> The developers forget that <i>Dinosaur</i> is actually a huge category of animals with a lot of variety spanning almost 200 million years, a time during which no species existed for much more than 20 million years. Even if the Mokole were dinosaurs, they would need the rite to change animal type from time to time way before the K-Pg event.<br /> <br /> Personally I find it more interesting to think of them as using dinosaurs in place of humans instead of for their &quot;animal&quot; forms, and the image of a titanic crocodile preying on a T-Rex is just too cool for me to ignore, I'd rather have the croc being the Mokole than the Rex. Especially when we start to have a lot of even non-avian dinos having feathers. It also restores the idea of the Mokole as a very distinct Fera, instead of the weird notion that their old animal forms had more kinship to the Corax than to the modern Mokole.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554697#post1554697\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The books kind of jump around on this, too. It might be conflating it with the Curse when it does.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think so.<br /> <br /> There's also some general notion across the WoD that contact with the supernatural is too scarring to humans even if they forget.<br /> <br /> But I think the idea of the <i>Delirium</i> as a protection is interesting to at least have around, use it or leave it as it fits best the story.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554698#post1554698\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But that's going a bit beyond the issue of how children relate to the effect.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's my main point.<br /> <br /> Regardless of the true nature of <i>Delirium</i>, if it is ever to be known (I don't think it has to), I can't see a reason for children to not be affected.<br /> <br /> My first concern is thematic, but the themes behind the Mists or ghosts are very different from the themes around <i>Delirium</i>. A child that doesn't flinch to a giant murder machine doesn't seem innocent, they seem broken. It would fit a <i>Widderslainte</i> more than a normal child. As a storytelling device it also seems weird and unnecessary, you already have kinfolk children, having every child immune doesn't seems to help a Werewolf game.<br /> <br /> Only after that I even consider possible explanations, but even then I don't see any reason for a child to not be affected. They're human, both physically and spiritually, just as any adult, they do feel fear, specially the more &quot;instinctive&quot;, intuitive kinds, and however it's explained the <i>Delirium</i> fits the bill for &quot;Primal Fear&quot;. I don't see any reason for children to not have it.<br /> <br /> Another approach that may ease things a little in favor of them being immune is to keep in mind that they're still scared shitless of those things, like any normal person would be, but what isn't affected is their memories of the event. But even then I'm not sure if this helps the game in any way. The idea of scarring children <b><i>specifically</i></b> with PTSD after a little Garou action seems weird and unnecessary in WtA, and something that brings particular concerns that actually detract from the game. Those children <i>reliably </i>growing to remember the events is even worse. This will just add the pressure for PCs to not change when there are children among the witnesses, and that's not the point of WtA.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-28T11:00:51-07:00","id":1554733,"title":"","content":"I wasn't saying that they wouldn't flinch.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-28T15:21:03-07:00","id":1554746,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samuelalpha86</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554733#post1554733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wasn't saying that they wouldn't flinch.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just to understand, what being immune to the <i>Delirium</i> would effectively mean in the game for you?<br /> <br /> How would it play out and what effect would it have in the game?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-29T13:05:58-07:00","id":1554788,"title":"","content":"What I mean is that it wouldn't give them an irrational reaction or amnesia.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-29T13:27:58-07:00","id":1554789,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554713#post1554713\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I didn't read SD yet, and it has its own problems, but I know the idea of them being dinosaurs was the original point and the book plays to that.<br /> <br /> But it's not only a contrived thing, and possibly less interesting (at least it is for me), most importantly it doesn't really means they were every dinosaur species, or that every dinosaur was kinfolk.<br /> <br /> The developers forget that <i>Dinosaur</i> is actually a huge category of animals with a lot of variety spanning almost 200 million years, a time during which no species existed for much more than 20 million years. Even if the Mokole were dinosaurs, they would need the rite to change animal type from time to time way before the K-Pg event.<br /> <br /> Personally I find it more interesting to think of them as using dinosaurs in place of humans instead of for their &quot;animal&quot; forms, and the image of a titanic crocodile preying on a T-Rex is just too cool for me to ignore, I'd rather have the croc being the Mokole than the Rex. Especially when we start to have a lot of even non-avian dinos having feathers. It also restores the idea of the Mokole as a very distinct Fera, instead of the weird notion that their old animal forms had more kinship to the Corax than to the modern Mokole.</div> </div> </div> </div>They certainly had enough time to cover a lot of mesozoic fauna, even if not all of the Dinosauria and their contemporaries among other groups of Reptilia*. And they would have been doing it at a slow pace, as species grew out of existing ones and others went extinct. Then they lost all of the non-avian groups in the WonderWork, and Gaia presumably made the birds into something else along with the snakes, leaving Mokole with a lot of Lepidosaurs, one admittedly very diverse group of Archosaurs, and turtles (which were then lost!).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-29T13:44:15-07:00","id":1554793,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554789#post1554789\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> They certainly had enough time to cover a lot of mesozoic fauna, even if not all of the Dinosauria and their contemporaries among other groups of Reptilia*. And they would have been doing it at a slow pace, as species grew out of existing ones and others went extinct. Then they lost all of the non-avian groups in the WonderWork, and Gaia presumably made the birds into something else along with the snakes, leaving Mokole with a lot of Lepidosaurs, one admittedly very diverse group of Archosaurs, and turtles (which were then lost!).</div> </div> </div> </div>Weren't the Nagas originally a subgroup of Mokole?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-29T21:04:45-07:00","id":1554820,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samuelalpha86</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554788#post1554788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I mean is that it wouldn't give them an irrational reaction or amnesia.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, but what would that do in the game?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554789#post1554789\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They certainly had enough time to cover a lot of mesozoic fauna</div> </div> </div> </div>They did, I just don't think they would do it, because that seems kind of pointless. Covering as many animal species as possible isn't the point, is it?<br /> <br /> But then again, my point is more a matter of finding them being linked to reptiles from the get-go more interesting. And equally more interesting to have them related to Dinosaurs because of the then non-existing human side, as it is the thing that really didn't existed by then and I simply find it more fun.<br /> <br /> Even without supernatural/fictional shenanigans, the time of the Dinosaurs was long enough for anything to be possible for the Mokole.\u200b<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samuelalpha86</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554793#post1554793\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Weren't the Nagas originally a subgroup of Mokole?</div> </div> </div> </div>No.<br /> <br /> They're their own thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-29T23:51:20-07:00","id":1554830,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554820#post1554820\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No.<br /> <br /> They're their own thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Now, yes<br /> But even the original breed books and earlier editions - then the few last ones, like Shattered Dreams reached back to this - mentioned they were in the same family of Dragon Kings the Mokol\u00e9 descended from<br /> These Kings who later became the Wani sacrificed Mnesis in order to hunt and to punish those who would betray Gaia and later became great dragon-spirits of water and venom and took their own descendants under their wings who became the Nagah<br /> Similarly the original idea was the Corax also branched from the Mokol\u00e9, trading the past for the present &quot;gossips&quot; and messages, but their connection to the Mnesis remained in the form of the Gifts when they consume the eye to gain some memories and knowledge/last sight of the corpse<br /> It never really went anywhere - like the mention of the Marsupial Kings - but was a fun lore tidbit","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-30T08:51:27-07:00","id":1554850,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554830#post1554830\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now, yes</div> </div> </div> </div>It's hard to say for sure how things were a fuckton of years ago. This is mostly legend by now, as Mnesis can't really say for sure how things were back then.<br /> <br /> And keep in mind that the &quot;Dragon Kings&quot; are about as related with each other as all mammals, or even less. It's like saying every mammalian Changer is actually the same thing.<br /> <br /> We could have dozens of completely unrelated groups of Fera, all tied to the Dragon Kings. It's weird to have all the land-based non-mammalian Changers be originally the same thing, but mammals are all this variety.<br /> <br /> Heck, one reason for Mnesis to work so badly for things before the Wonder-Work could very well be that those modern Changers actually aren't really descendants of the Dragon Kings, just somehow partial inheritors to their memories.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-30T11:05:05-07:00","id":1554856,"title":"","content":"Then again, no other changers get to have a unique-built form the way that the Mokole do. The way things are now is likely a setup to justify that in some way, with the remnants of prehistoric reptile people skipping across sometimes what would be vast differences in descent to change their forms.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-30T12:21:29-07:00","id":1554864,"title":"","content":"Tell me about it...<br /> After the Age of Kings, <b>Ammonite </b><b>shifters</b> (who were at war with the Rokea) and <b>bristlecreepers</b> went extinct along with their Kin (Breed Book: Mokol\u00e9 p.21)<br /> The <b>Ammonite </b>I can picture, they are probably in some way or a nother related to the the <b>Qyrall</b> described in the Rokea breedbook<br /> <br /> &quot;You know what an anemone is, right? There was a time when anemones were not rooted to one spot the way they are now. Instead, they had a snail-like foot under them, and they oozed across the ocean floor. They were also much bigger than they are now, and their arms were strong enough to hold and poision a Rokea, if we got thatclose. These creatures- called the Qyrall, after their mother - used sand and theri own ooze to build up fortresses underwater, using knowledge that Qyrl stole from C'et. They would ride the leviathans and use Qyrl's gifts of stealth to block out the massive brutes' presence until they were right on top of a slew, then change in to an<b> almost-humanoid</b> shape to do battle&quot;<br /> <br /> Sure an Ammonite and an Anemone are two very different things, but at least have some connection via Qyrl, the creator of squid, octopi, cuttlefish, slugs, snails, and other boneless sea creatures\u200b<br /> <br /> But what the hell is a &quot;bristlecreeper&quot;?<br /> No, really. What is it?<br /> <br /> I <i>think </i>they were primitive mammals like the Eomia (<b> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eomaia\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eomaia</a> ) </b>or something along the line<b>, </b>maybe some spiritual ancestor of the Ratling and other mammalian shifters, like the Khara were the Bastet ancestor (used to be at least) and the new editions suggested there was a bridge between the Khara and the Mokol\u00e9, some common ancestor of the reptiles and mammals, lan early synapsid ancestor and the Khara had some connection to the Mnesis of the Mokol\u00e9 (they removed things that shoudn't be remembered, while the Mokol\u00e9 gathered the things to remember... pretty cool concept)<br /> Still, after... decades? I still have the foggiest idea what the hell a bristlecreeper shifter could have been or looked like<br /> <br /> But I am starting to drift away from the original topic about delirium, sorry \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-31T13:11:06-07:00","id":1554897,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554864#post1554864\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But what the hell is a &quot;bristlecreeper&quot;?<br /> No, really. What is it?<br /> <br /> I <i>think </i>they were primitive mammals like the Eomia (<b> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eomaia\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eomaia</a> ) </b>or something along the line<b>, </b>maybe some spiritual ancestor of the Ratling and other mammalian shifters, like the Khara were the Bastet ancestor (used to be at least) and the new editions suggested there was a bridge between the Khara and the Mokol\u00e9, some common ancestor of the reptiles and mammals, lan early synapsid ancestor and the Khara had some connection to the Mnesis of the Mokol\u00e9 (they removed things that shoudn't be remembered, while the Mokol\u00e9 gathered the things to remember... pretty cool concept)<br /> Still, after... decades? I still have the foggiest idea what the hell a bristlecreeper shifter could have been or looked like<br /> <br /> But I am starting to drift away from the original topic about delirium, sorry</div> </div> </div> </div>It would be unlikely to be a common antecedent to mammals and reptiles. The most recent examples of those were Anthracosaurs, and they would not have had fur or other neat (and plesiomorphic) surface features. Besides, you don't need to have a changing breed be closely related to another to have them be helpers in a given niche. Case in point, Ajaba and Garou, and Garou with a lot of other breeds that they killed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-31T16:56:31-07:00","id":1554903,"title":"","content":"The relationships between <i>Changing Breeds</i> are ultimately cultural and spiritual. Sometimes the writers got carried out in their exploration of pseudo-biology and pseudo-evolution, but those are not really how the <i>Changing Breeds</i> work.<br /> <br /> At the end of the day they're people, even before people existed.<br /> <br /> For the <b><u>bristlecreepers</u></b> being common ancestors, it doesn't make a lot of sense. The <i>Dragon Kings</i> are directly identified with clades that appeared at least 50 million years after the last common ancestor of Synapsides and Sauropsides. So even if such a creature had a Changing Breed, it would have been long extinct before the Mokol\u00e9 appeared for the first time.<br /> <br /> In the end the game is really focused on the last thousand years, 20k at most, well within the time of modern humans (~50k). Everything at a longer time scale we don't know even if the Changing Breeds that existed were already the ones we know now, save the Mokol\u00e9, Rokea and Ananasi.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-01T20:05:32-07:00","id":1554931,"title":"","content":"I don't have children be resistant to the Delerium. I think it's fundamentally different than the Fog or Mists even though the mechanics are very similar.<br /> <br /> However, because I assume that almost all children will only have a Willpower of 1 or 2, I almost always have them be affected by the Curse while their parents (and often their older siblings) do not while in the presence of a Garou in their homid form. In that way, they almost are always able to detect that &quot;something is wrong&quot; with the Garou even though they don't know what's wrong. An adult will almost always rationalize the effects of the Curse on them (when it does happen), but children don't know any better. So they are prone to wild speculation based on imagination, superstition, and other stories.<br /> <br /> At the same time, the game says that certain populations aren't as affected by the Delerium as others. There are people who can see through the Delerium. So I wouldn't be adverse to having <i>some</i> children (or whoever) also be able to see through it. It would definitely be the exception, not the rule, but it could happen. The rare exception can be used as a means to introduce a new plot or other element into the game.<br /> <br /> One thing I hate is when players feel the Delerium is their Get Out of Jail Free card when it comes to the Veil so they can go into Crinos whenever they want. I always tell them that 1) there is always a non-trivial chance people will not forget what they see (at least 14%), 2) there are various people/creatures who are always immune, and 3) there's always more stuff you don't know about and can't control. So be smart about when you transform into Crinos.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-02T07:57:57-08:00","id":1554960,"title":"","content":"Cameras are one thing that you have to watch out for when going Crinos.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-02T15:03:12-08:00","id":1554989,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samuelalpha86</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554960#post1554960\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Cameras are one thing that you have to watch out for when going Crinos.</div> </div> </div> </div>For a lot of Garou, sure, but they have more control over tech overall than vampires as a group. Bricking a modern camera is also far more effective and easy than it used to be; in 1995, if you did that, it would still be on a roll of film that could be developed at a local camera and video shop. Now? If the device is bricked and the digital storage follows, even if they've taken a picture already, it's gone. Beware the cloud for the meantime, but most data centers are trying to get money by setting up LLM space and shoving everything out, so that capacity might be out of reach in some places.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-05T15:38:49-08:00","id":1555140,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1554746#post1554746\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Just to understand, what being immune to the <i>Delirium</i> would effectively mean in the game for you?<br /> <br /> How would it play out and what effect would it have in the game?</div> </div> </div> </div>The way I mean it would work is that it would be similar to how Native Americans and Asians are resistant to the Delirium, however they would still be subject to the Curse. Later on in life they may look back on the encounter as just their imagination.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-05T16:13:01-08:00","id":1555142,"title":"","content":"Uh... there's no ethnic base Delirium resistance","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-05T18:01:15-08:00","id":1555144,"title":"","content":"IIRC the last time that was mentioned in a W:tA book it still had the caveat of &quot;you must be raised in this culture to get this&quot;. I think it was also geared more toward Australia than Asia.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-05T18:17:57-08:00","id":1555146,"title":"","content":"It was Native Americans, Asians, Aborigines and some tribes in the Amazon who weren't subjected to the Impergium so they were immune to the Delirium.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28613,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","user":"Samuelalpha86","date":". 11-15-2025, 11:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-05T18:39:34-08:00","id":1555149,"title":"","content":"&quot;Members of cultures that didn\u2019t suffer the Impergium to a great degree (such as Native Americans and Aboriginal Australians) might also be granted a bonus, at the Storyteller\u2019s discretion \u2014 though only if they\u2019ve been raised without contact with other cultures.&quot;<br /> <br /> So, lets be clear about two things:<br /> <br /> 1) <b>Might</b> be granted at ST discretion, not automatic<br /> 2) Doesn't apply if raised with contact with other cultures, which basically negates the entire thing from mattering. There's only an estimated 10K indigenous people that live in isolation in the whole world (including a few that live on small islands)<br /> <br /> If you're setting the game in the distant past? OK, this might actually matter. Otherwise this isn't really going to be a thing. The vast majority of indigenous peoples do not live in no-contact conditions (there are zero in North America or Australia).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-05T23:59:10-08:00","id":1555154,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1555149#post1555149\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;Members of cultures that didn\u2019t suffer the Impergium to a great degree (such as Native Americans and Aboriginal Australians) might also be granted a bonus, at the Storyteller\u2019s discretion \u2014 though only if they\u2019ve been raised without contact with other cultures.&quot;<br /> <br /> So, lets be clear about two things:<br /> <br /> 1) <b>Might</b> be granted at ST discretion, not automatic<br /> 2) Doesn't apply if raised with contact with other cultures, which basically negates the entire thing from mattering. There's only an estimated 10K indigenous people that live in isolation in the whole world (including a few that live on small islands)<br /> <br /> If you're setting the game in the distant past? OK, this might actually matter. Otherwise this isn't really going to be a thing. The vast majority of indigenous peoples do not live in no-contact conditions (there are zero in North America or Australia).</div> </div> </div> </div>To be fair, it not really made sanse from that certain point of view<br /> It is either a racial memory thing, or racial &quot;curse&quot;, Shapeshifter effect... or not<br /> The &quot;my ancestors were mass murdered&quot; excuse not really works because more often than not whole areas were depopulated during the history , tribes, cultures got lost and no descendants remained - and it circles back to the racial memory or supernatural effects and just because you not really had contact with others it shouldn't make you &quot;immune&quot; or more resistant, unless....<br /> (dun dun duuuuun)<br /> <br /> This is a sense reaches back to one of my favorite topics - touching a bit the vampire for a moment - about a pre-adamic race<br /> \u201cGenesis 4:17 tells us, \u201cCain knew his wife, and she conceived and bore Enoch.\u201d That is the first and last mention of the wife of Cain in the entire Bible. Naturally, this raises some questions: Who was she, and where did she come from?<br /> if you just read the beginning of Genesis, it sounds like there are plenty of people around....enough where Cain is worried people will kill him. Enough where there was a land of Nod where he found a wife and built a city.<br /> And Genesis also told us &quot;God blessed them and said to them, \u201cBe fruitful and increase in number; fill the earth and subdue it&quot;<br /> <i>&quot;Then</i> the LORD God formed a man from the dust of the ground and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life, and the man became a living being.8 Now the LORD God had planted a garden in the east, in Eden; and there he put the man he had formed&quot;<br /> <br /> Some see the verses above as evidence or implication that there was a pre-Adamic race of mankind already living outside of the Garden prior to Adam's creation and placement in Eden. Seems to me you need a lot of people to build a city. Also, it sounds like Adam and Eve didn't have any more children until after Cain killed Abel. So narratively, there were other people around outside of those created by Adam and Eve.<br /> <br /> The Bible says Adam was the first man and Eve is the mother of all living. Apart from that, it is silent on whether or not God created other humans outside of the garden. You might ask, &quot;how could Eve be the mother of all living if there were other humans created outside of the garden?&quot; It's actually simple: Only descendants of Noah survived the flood, and Noah was a direct descendant of Eve. The issue with there being humans not related to Adam and Eve is that it then suggests there are humans not infected with sin introduced by Adam. They wouldn\u2019t need redemption because sin was never passed down to them by Adam. So\u2026 why not cut off the line of Adam and Eve and keep the other pure humans? You say that the decedents of Adam and Eve were the only ones to survive the flood\u2026 but why? They would literally be the only actually in need of redemption. Why was everyone else sinful? Did all these other humans commit another original sin?<br /> <br /> Lets not go deeper, because the answer is not realted to the topic, but the point is having &quot;separate&quot; branches of humanity, having qulatities the others not had is something that always lingered in the background and White Wolf sometimes touched this with its games<br /> <br /> So cultures MAY or may not immune is based on the &quot;they are different... somehow&quot; and that begs the question? Why?<br /> They escaped the racial memory? Mokol\u00e9 removed their ancestors and their whole Memory from the mnesis to spare them? (Possible)<br /> A different branch of humans that never been harassed?<br /> Deep dive in lore and theories, but at the end of the day as <b><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\">Heavy Arms</a></b> said? Eh... not really matters nowdays<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-06T04:48:56-08:00","id":1555161,"title":"","content":"OK... for the sake of us with religious educations from childhood that include being trained in Biblical Hebrew and being decently informed on critical scholarship of the Torah/Hebrew Bible/Old Testament... please avoid &quot;the scriptures tell us that...&quot; because they most likely don't.<br /> <br /> Also, the answers are really simple:<br /> <br /> 1) Genesis contains two different creation stories (the cosmic creation story of the days of creation, and the hands on creation story of Eden) that were merged together kinda messily as Judaism moved from a henoistic religion, with multiple deities within a larger pantheon even if they weren't all honored, and into monotheism. The gaps are because the actions of two different divine beings are being smooshed into a single narrative. Ancient cultures are fully of the hands on creation myths where the first humans are made and then things just skip to there being lots of humans. It's not a sign of some other lineage out there, it's just a common early mythological narrative device.<br /> <br /> 2) The original sin narrative is a far later invention that only adds to the mess that was created in point 1. Adam and Eve also eat the fruit of the knowledge of good and the knowledge of evil before they got around to making any more humans (and they had a third son, Seth). By the original narrative this is supposed to mean all humans have this knowledge in them. Because they're the original humans however you want to square their boys finding wives. And remember that whenever the old books are talking about redemption, they're not talking about spiritual salvation from sin. The word is being used in the same sense as redeeming a coupon. The Lord is redeeming <b>their people</b> from being servants of other deities after they got taken to other lands. The Lord is getting their property back from the deities currently in possession of it, so they can go back to being loyal followers the Lord's land and so on. Sin exists because humans know of good and evil, the Eden story is just a myth about how that knowledge came to be. It's not an implication of some other spiritually separate group of humans; and even if those existed they'd be childishly ignorant like Adam and Eve before eating the fruit.<br /> <br /> There's no deep mystery here, there's no need for complicated explanations. Just a need to remember that scripture is a collection of stories from across time with multiple authors and editors.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-06T13:04:45-08:00","id":1555171,"title":"","content":"Not to say that &quot;Salvation&quot; in the Hebrew Bible meant being saved from problems and turmoil in life, not any spiritual thing. But that has zero to do with the topic at hand.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1555154#post1555154\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> To be fair, it not really made sanse from that certain point of view<br /> It is either a racial memory thing, or racial &quot;curse&quot;, Shapeshifter effect... or not<br /> The &quot;my ancestors were mass murdered&quot; excuse not really works because more often than not whole areas were depopulated during the history , tribes, cultures got lost and no descendants remained - and it circles back to the racial memory or supernatural effects and just because you not really had contact with others it shouldn't make you &quot;immune&quot; or more resistant, unless....<br /> (dun dun duuuuun)<br /> <br /> This is a sense reaches back to one of my favorite topics - touching a bit the vampire for a moment - about a pre-adamic race</div> </div> </div> </div>First, I would recommend you look for actual bible scholars for your topic, I think you'll actually like it a lot more after that. I'd recommend starting with the videos from Dan McClellan on YouTube, but he has good indications of actual academic books.<br /> <br /> Most important, though, WtA doesn't work with this at all. It works with evolution and Natural History.<br /> <br /> The idea of some particular people not suffering <i>Delirium</i> in the same degree comes from the notion that the <i>Impergium</i> happened at a point in time <i>after</i> the dispersion of humanity, and only where wolves lived, as there were no Garou away from wolves. Keeping in mind that the <b>Bunnyip</b> weren't originally Thylacines, they became them later.<br /> <br /> With this in mind, the <i>Impergium</i> would have happened later than 40k years ago, if we assume South American population coming from Polynesia, or 15k years ago if we assume it coming from the north. Both possibilities still put the <i>Impergium</i> thousands of years before written records, and way before the creation of humanity according to literal readings of scripture (something between the end of the 4th millennium to the end of the 6th millennium depending on source, so at most 8k years ago).<br /> <br /> And Werewolf consistently works with this time frame, even if not directly stated in the legends. The <i>Impergium</i> was at least 9k years ago, probably longer, and <i>lasted</i> for thousands of years.<br /> <br /> Strict biblical interpretation, especially any interpretation that thinks it to be clear on anything, is hardly at home in Vampire, it has no bearing in most other game lines.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samuelalpha86</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1555140#post1555140\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The way I mean it would work is that it would be similar to how Native Americans and Asians are resistant to the Delirium, however they would still be subject to the Curse. Later on in life they may look back on the encounter as just their imagination.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not exactly my question, I'm not yet asking how you want to mechanically implement it.<br /> <br /> I mean, what you expect to happen in the story, how you think it will play out? How a scene with a child that's immune to the <i>Delirium</i> will play out, and how will it impact the experiences and decisions of the PCs? How it affects the story being told?<br /> <br /> What will change besides the character being nominally immune to the <i>Delirium</i>?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-06T21:29:53-08:00","id":1555198,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Doesn't apply if raised with contact with other cultures, </div> </div> </div>I think it's pretty clear that the Garou explanation is, as it often is in an oral history fully suffused with metaphors and tutelary fables, broadly incorrect. The delirium is clearly a cultural contagion, much like L. Bob Rife's Snowcrash virus. The Impergium is related but clearly is not the cause of the Delirium. <br /> <br /> Also <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6716607/\" target=\"_blank\">https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6716607/</a> turns out a lot of evidence of instinctive fears in babies is overestimated.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27296-devidramoth","name":"Devidramoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27296_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-07T10:08:22-08:00","id":1555208,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Devidramoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1555198#post1555198\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think it's pretty clear that the Garou explanation is, as it often is in an oral history fully suffused with metaphors and tutelary fables, broadly incorrect. The delirium is clearly a cultural contagion, much like L. Bob Rife's Snowcrash virus. The Impergium is related but clearly is not the cause of the Delirium.<br /> <br /> Also <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6716607/\" target=\"_blank\">https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6716607/</a> turns out a lot of evidence of instinctive fears in babies is overestimated.</div> </div> </div> </div>What are you, the W5 corebook? Humans managed to pass on notable and broadly correct information that way for over a hundred thousand years, so with access to Gift-enhanced memory and clawing glyphs into things, the Garou probably managed a lot better.<br /> <br /> (Also, if Garou are no longer responsible for the aversion of humanity to them, that changes a lot of the atmosphere of the game, notably making humanity almost entirely to blame for everything bad that's happened. Choose your explanations carefully.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-07T15:15:42-08:00","id":1555218,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Devidramoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium?p=1555198#post1555198\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think it's pretty clear that the Garou explanation is, as it often is in an oral history fully suffused with metaphors and tutelary fables, broadly incorrect. The delirium is clearly a cultural contagion, much like L. Bob Rife's Snowcrash virus. The Impergium is related but clearly is not the cause of the Delirium.<br /> <br /> Also <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6716607/\" target=\"_blank\">https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6716607/</a> turns out a lot of evidence of instinctive fears in babies is overestimated.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a lot to unpack.<br /> <br /> Oral history is a complex phenomenon. Calling it &quot;broadly incorrect&quot; is a simplification that misses the point so completely as to actually be broadly incorrect.<br /> <br /> It's more correct to say they're hard to interpret and mixed with interpretation, interpolations and influences.<br /> <br /> But more importantly, there's nothing clear about the <i>Delirium</i>, and that is intentional. Mind you, the aforementioned rule is optional for a reason, it isn't meant to be how the game works by default, and it doesn't imply anything. The very concept of &quot;cultural contagion&quot; don't actually answers anything. Is it a biological phenomenon? Spiritual? Soul-related? In Snow Crash it is biological, but it is also very specific in where it came from and how it works.<br /> <br /> You can run this as a basis for a chronicle, but it's anything but &quot;clear&quot; as far as setting discussions go.<br /> <br /> And the reality behind &quot;instinctive fear&quot; is mostly irrelevant. This is a fictional world. Things may be true there even if they aren't in the real world, besides the fact that they may be supernatural in nature.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-09T02:45:37-08:00","id":1557411,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samuelalpha86</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554669-are-children-resistant-to-the-delirium#post1554669\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Due to their open mindedness, innocence and inability to comprehend the fear of death can Children resist the Delirium?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Delirium is generational PTSD that was burned into humans on the genetic level. It's hard wired into our instincts. Our very DNA cries out when it sees a werewolf doing werewolf things.<br /> <br /> Only someone with extraordinarily high willpower can suppress the urge to panic in that situation. Even those who are capable of fighting back, do so in a crazed, berserker state.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29027-greathornedratkin","name":"GreatHornedRatkin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29027_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2026"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}